 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Receiver and RTK",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Built on the u-blox F9 platform, the module tracks two bands per constellation and computes RTK fixes from RTCM corrections (an OSR solution) or PPP-RTK fixes from SPARTN or CLAS corrections (an SSR solution), reaching centimetre-level position."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Moving base",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Both base and rover may move; the rover then computes the vector between the two antennas. Uses named are a drone following its owner or landing on a moving platform, and attitude sensing with two modules on one vehicle or tool. For this mode identical antennas mounted in the same orientation are advised, as it keeps phase-centre variation small. Heading accuracy improves as the baseline grows."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Constellations",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "GPS, GLONASS, Galileo and BeiDou are received at once, together with SBAS and QZSS, on both the L1 and L2/E5b bands. A smaller set of constellations can be configured to save power. QZSS shares GPS frequencies and is usable only with GPS enabled. Using both bands needs matching hardware design on the board. AssistNow Online assistance is supported."
  },
  {
   "topic": "QZSS and SBAS",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "QZSS L1C/A and L2C are tracked alongside GPS. The QZSS L1S signal carrying SLAS pseudorange corrections (computed by Japanese ground stations, sent on 1575.42 MHz) can be used. SBAS covers WAAS, EGNOS, QZSS L1Sb and GAGAN; using it improves position in those regions but worsens timing accuracy because of an extra inter-system time calibration."
  },
  {
   "topic": "DGNSS and corrections",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "RTK needs RTCM version 3 messages; DGNSS follows RTCM 10403.3. As a rover the module decodes the RTCM 3.3 and SPARTN 2.0.1 messages listed; as a base station it produces the RTCM 3.3 output messages listed. For CLAS, a NEO-D9C sends UBX-RXM-QZSSL6 messages with the QZSS L6 corrections into any port; CLAS corrects GPS, Galileo and QZSS satellites visible in Japan."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Raw data",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "All broadcast navigation data from tracked satellites, including QZSS and SBAS, comes out in UBX-RXM-SFRBX; RRLP-compatible satellite information can also be produced. UBX-RXM-RAWX gives carrier phase, pseudorange, Doppler and quality for every supported signal, in RINEX 3 conventions, once bit sync and time of week are established."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Antenna",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "An active antenna must be used. VCC_RF supplies it (or an external LNA), ANT_OFF can switch it off, and ANT_DETECT and ANT_SHORT_N report its presence and a short. Total gain ahead of RF_IN must be 17 to 50 dB."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interface selection",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Pins 42 to 45 carry UART1 and I2C when D_SEL is open or high, and SPI when D_SEL is low; I2C is unavailable in SPI mode. Every input has an internal pull-up and may be left open when unused. All PIO levels follow VCC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "UART",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Baud rate is configurable from 9600 to 921600 bit/s. There is no hardware flow control. UART1 is the host port. UART2 (RXD2, TXD2) is meant for corrections and must not be the only host link: firmware update, safeboot and backup modes are not available on it, and it sends no boot screen. Because the default NMEA output reports every band received, the chosen baud rate has to carry the load at the selected navigation rate."
  },
  {
   "topic": "SPI",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Off by default; enabled by pulling D_SEL low. Peripheral mode only, clock up to 5.5 MHz, transfers up to 125 kB/s, default CPOL = 0 and CPHA = 0. Timing is specified at SCK = 5.33 MHz."
  },
  {
   "topic": "I2C",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Peripheral only, Fast-mode up to 400 kbit/s; Standard-mode is not supported. The module may stretch the clock by holding SCL low, for at most 20 ms, which lowers throughput."
  },
  {
   "topic": "USB",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "USB 2.0 full speed (12 Mbit/s) for a host, powered through V_USB. Certification of the USB port may not be possible because of how it is built."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Backup supply",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "V_BCKP (1.65 to 3.6 V) keeps backup data with VCC removed, drawing 45 µA typical at 3 V. Software backup mode draws 1.4 mA typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Ordering and firmware",
   "page": 23,
   "text": "Ordering code ZED-F9P-04B, type number ZED-F9P-04B-01, ships with firmware FW 1.00 HPG 1.32. The B grade is the professional grade."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Footprint keep-out",
   "page": 21,
   "text": "Up to 0.30 mm of de-paneling tab residue (L) can stand out from the body edges; its shape and position vary, and the keep-out area round the module has to allow for it."
  }
 ],
